WEBVTT

00:00:00.022 --> 00:00:07.592
Our classroom example begins with a CSV, but real RAG projects usually start with other data sources.

00:00:07.592 --> 00:00:14.407
A company might have PDFs in SharePoint, Word documents in a shared drive, web pages, database records,

00:00:14.407 --> 00:00:18.627
support tickets, object storage, or internal APIs.

00:00:18.627 --> 00:00:23.712
Before RAG can search those sources, the content has to be extracted and normalized.

00:00:23.712 --> 00:00:28.427
For PDFs, that may mean a parser such as PyMuPDF.

00:00:28.427 --> 00:00:31.602
Scanned documents may require OCR.

00:00:31.602 --> 00:00:34.802
Web pages need HTML cleanup.

00:00:34.802 --> 00:00:38.052
A database may already have structured metadata that should be preserved.

00:00:38.052 --> 00:00:45.572
[tts-break] The important point is that this ingestion layer comes before chunking and embeddings.

00:00:45.572 --> 00:00:54.152
Once the text and metadata have been extracted, the rest of the workflow is the same as our

00:00:54.152 --> 00:00:58.197
classroom example: clean the text, create chunks, embed them, store the vectors,

00:00:58.197 --> 00:01:02.552
and retrieve them when a user asks a question.

00:01:02.552 --> 00:01:08.240
So the CSV is a simplification of the ingestion stage, not a different architecture.
